description:
[narrowed from route-edit-88e970c2] In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/self-interference-scan.ts, detect tests that the verify gate blames on unrelated drafts, so a state-dependent test stops being attributed to whatever draft happens to be composing.

WHY. feature_compose blocks a draft on tests failing now that were not failing at baseline, and re-runs the suite once keeping only failures present in both runs. That correctly sheds random flake, but a test whose result depends on mutable live state fails deterministically on BOTH runs, so reproduction is treated as proof the draft caused it. Measured over 250 compose reports: 104 reached the test gate, 13 were blamed for draft-introduced test failures, and all 13 blamed the SAME test, across mutually unrelated drafts. So 100 percent of that rejection class is false. The signature is decisive and already sits in durable evidence this resolver reads: one test name appearing in the blame detail of reports for several DIFFERENT gaps cannot have been caused by any of those drafts. This resolver already scans every compose report and already turns each new incident kind into one substrateGap, so it only needs to recognise the pattern.

THE CHANGE. Exactly one op. Find this line, which occurs exactly once in the file:

  let gaps: Array<{ id?: string; summary?: string; classification_metadata?: { resolution_commits?: unknown[]; approach_decisions?: Array<{ outcome?: unknown; at?: string }> } }> = [];

Insert the following block IMMEDIATELY BEFORE that line, keeping that line unchanged and last:

  try {
    const blameFiles = await readdir("/workspace/proposals");
    const testToGaps = new Map<string, Set<string>>();
    const suffix = "-compose-report.json";
    for (const bname of blameFiles) {
      if (!bname.endsWith(suffix)) continue;
      const bgap = bname.slice(0, bname.length - suffix.length);
      let btext = "";
      try { btext = await Bun.file("/workspace/proposals/" + bname).text(); } catch { continue; }
      const bmark = "NEW test failures introduced by this draft";
      const bat = btext.indexOf(bmark);
      if (bat < 0) continue;
      for (const piece of btext.slice(bat, bat + 3000).split("(fail)").slice(1)) {
        const tname = String(piece.split(" ; ")[0] ?? "").split("[")[0].trim();
        if (tname.length < 8) continue;
        const bset = testToGaps.get(tname) ?? new Set<string>();
        bset.add(bgap);
        testToGaps.set(tname, bset);
      }
    }
    for (const [tname, bset] of testToGaps) {
      if (bset.size < 3) continue;
      if (incidents.length < cap * 3) incidents.push({ kind: "state_dependent_test_blame", id: tname.slice(0, 80), detail: ("this test was blamed as draft-introduced across " + bset.size + " DIFFERENT gaps, so no draft caused it: it depends on mutable live state and fails deterministically on the confirmation re-run, which the gate reads as proof. Exclude it from the newTest blame set rather than rejecting the drafts.").slice(0, 400) });
    }
  } catch { }

SCOPE. One insertion only. Do not modify the gaps declaration line, the return body, or any existing incident kind. The threshold of three distinct gaps is deliberate: a genuinely draft-introduced failure appears under one gap and must NOT be flagged.

